---
/**
 * P4 Development partners & NGOs: /partner-with-us/development-partners/ (T3, implements doc 04 matrix 4.1).
 * C1 to C14 in order, as the numbered table in doc 06 P4, without C3 (Cost of Waiting) and C4 (Imagine), which the audience pages leave to Home since WP47.
 * WP30c (owner direction 2026-10-14, "less is more"): every CMF band stays, each states its point once. Gone: the In Short summary, the fit check, the evidence-and-fit
 * brief, the 2015 pilot quote with its caveat, the second survey of the standard inclusions and the restated three-step mechanism. Five FAQs, not nine.
 * WP35 (cycle-12 review): the trial and the survey are shown once, as tiles in Results at a Glance; the proof band that restated them is a one-line strip of links (#proof-band). The WSDFM tile is gone
 * from the glance (its 71% is program records, read beside the trial's 63.1%; the Kosovo program result takes its place).
 * WP36 (cycle-13 reviews): each figure is stated once. The Kosovo result (102 of 150) is on its Proof in Practice card, so the glance's program-record tile is UNDP YES, Korail (PR-03: a program with UNDP, one of the development partners in PA-01)
 * and the Kosovo tile is gone; the mechanism card says "Placement support into ..."; the three pains are statements ("What Gets in the Way"); the cost question has the one-line route answer and a link to the hub FAQ.
 * WP37 (cycle-14 review): the women-and-marginalized-groups answer no longer restates the three program figures (Proof in Practice, directly above, shows them); it says so and keeps the scope of the trial.
 * WP39 (cycle-16 review): "What Your Portfolio Gets" is three short cards (IncludesBand), not the funder concern / what is included / practical benefit matrix.
 * WP40 (cycle-17 review): "Reporting and Review" says what the reports and reviews do instead of pointing at the overview; the closing "It doesn’t have to be this way." line of the cost band is gone.
 * WP42 (cycle-19 reviews): "Our Approach" is the two shared sentences with the tracking said as the cards say it ("Partners receive monthly reports and outcome tracking after each course, as agreed in the program scope."; POSITIONING.developmentPartners,
 * declared whole in scripts/check-facts.mjs), and the YouthWIDE explanation still follows it; the ">4×" tile (the glance's fourth) quotes the multiplier and the nine-trial average only (the +10.3 is the lead tile's: RC-06a's glance note).
 * WP43 (cycle-20 review): the cost-of-waiting heading is "Build Work Entry into the Training Budget" (the old one, "Training Without Placement Spends Budget Without Earnings", was an absolute claim; the two cards under it still carry the claims).
 * WP47 (owner decision 2026-10-15): the page opens with its own proof and goes straight to its offer. The Cost of Waiting band ("Build Work Entry into the Training Budget") and the Imagine band ("What Your Monthly Report
 * Could Show") are gone; Home carries them. Nothing on this page depended on them: the monthly reports, follow-up surveys and documentary evidence (where available) are stated in the "Questions Partners Ask"
 * answers, "Results Your Evaluators Can Check" and "Reporting and Review".
 * WP48 (cycle-23 review, item 25): the ">4×" tile's label says what is compared ("The employment effect for women offered a place, against the 2.3-point average across nine vocational-training trials"), so RC-06a's glance note
 * ("Against 2.3 percentage points on average ...") repeated it and is gone.
 * WP49 (cycle-24 review): the problem band is headed "Show Who Reaches Paid Work" and its first two cards "Training Must Connect to Paid Work" and "Barriers Can Interrupt Training" (the earlier headings stated sector-wide conclusions; the card bodies are unchanged);
 * "What Your Portfolio Gets" says in its first card that one delivery partner connects training, mentoring and placement support (the hours stay in the delivery model above); the ">4×" tile is the fourth tile of "Results at a Glance" (a
 * single tile needs no "More Results" heading); the closing steps band is "What Happens Next".
 * WP50 (cycle-25 review): the problem band's lead says "... keeping hard-to-reach groups engaged, is the hard part." (it said "... groups in, ...").
 * WP51 (cycle-26 review, Kimi P2): the Track Record band opens with the shared origin line, "Since 2014, from a Copenhagen launch to programs in 15 countries and territories." (ORIGIN_SHORT, as on Governments and Foundations), in place of
 * "Launched in Copenhagen in 2014."; PartnerGuideBand no longer swaps in the launch alone for a band that shows SC-03's tile (Investors prints the same shared line in its own band, InvestorGuide: Grok m1).
 * WP52 (cycle-27 review, Kimi M2): the ">4×" tile is gone from the glance, which is now three tiles in one row (the trial, the survey and UNDP YES, Korail); its comparison is the +10.3 tile's note ("More than four times the 2.3-point average across
 * nine vocational-training trials."). Foundations and Governments never showed the ">4×" tile, and Home's first slide keeps its own (there it is the slide's benchmark, not a repeat of a glance tile).
 * WP52 (cycle-27 reviews, Gemini): the "Where They Work" card says "Placement support into local, remote and global work, ..." (it said "... local, remote, overseas and freelance work", not the site's usual wording); the "Choose Where to Start" band's eyebrow is "Options"
 * (see the Governments page).
 * WP53 (owner follow-up, 2026-10-16): the "Where They Work" card says "local, remote and global work" once, in its title; the text is "Placement support and outcome tracking for 12 months after each course." (as Home's card, with the months from PD-02).
 * WP58 (cycle-30 reviews, Claude S10 and GPT-6.1 Sol G6): the third "What Your Portfolio Gets" card is "Progress Your Evaluators Can Check" (it was "Results Your Evaluators Can Check": baseline data, surveys and monthly reports are a reporting plan, not results);
 * Adrian Afraham's card shows his verbatim quotation, name and program without the one-line summary above it ("Secured a first freelance job after the Graphic Designing course, followed by a job offer"), which repeated the quotation.
 * WP62 (cycle-34 review, Grok): the UNDP YES tile of Results at a Glance names its year ("... in UNDP YES, Korail (2017), found their first job within three months"; the year is read from PR-03). The Impact page's tile keeps the register's label, because its note gives the year.
 * WP62 (cycle-34 review, Gemini): the "Where They Work" card is unchanged (it already says "for 12 months", read from PD-02); Home, the shared mechanism band and YouthWIDE now say it the same way.
 * WP65 (cycle-37 review, Gemini, Sonnet): the first "What Your Portfolio Gets" card reads "CodersTrust connects training, mentoring and placement support in one program." (it said "One delivery partner connects training, mentoring and placement support.").
 * The UNDP YES tile's label (WP62: PR-03's glance label with its year) is UNDP_YES_TILE_LABEL in partners.ts now, since Foundations' fourth glance tile is the same tile.
 * WP66 (owner request 2026-10-20): the Track Record band's list is the signed-agreement sentence for development partners and NGOs (SC-11c, `signed` on PartnerGuideBand: "CodersTrust has signed MOUs, agreements or contracts with ... development partners and NGOs."), under the two tiles;
 * the band's heading says "Since 2014", so the sentence leaves the year to it.
 * WP67 (cycle-38 review, Sonnet): the "Where They Work" card closes "..., as agreed in the program scope." (WHERE_THEY_WORK_TEXT, partners.ts, the same text Home, the shared mechanism band and YouthWIDE print).
 * WP175 (owner decisions of 28 November 2026: every conversion page follows CMF v3, C1 to C14, in order and coverage; each band is tagged with its component through the Tone wrapper):
 *   C1 hero · GEO (the route link under the hero is a second link of the delivery band) · GLANCE Results at a Glance (a recorded row, doc 06 P4 v1.23)
 *   C2–C4 the problem band, declared compressed (owner decision 2026-10-15, recast 2026-11-28), with its three ranked pains and the C3 transition line last
 *   C5 the track record: the empathy line, the one-line origin, the tiles and the signed agreements, the strips, the method line last · C6 the delivery model, the positioning statement first
 *   C7 Proof in Practice, led by the one-line trial lead (Q3 (a)), then Adrian Afraham's story (it sat before the problem band) · C8 one card per pain in rank order, a gain creator (the recognized credential)
 *   and How It Works in Three Steps · C9 the questions and the due-diligence view · C10 What Makes CodersTrust Different · C11 the offer · C12 "Start with a Pilot, Then Decide on Evidence" (restored), with
 *   the "Reporting and Review" callout under it · C13 · C14 the steps and the first-step line.
 */
import PartnerGlance from '../../../components/pages/partners/PartnerGlance.astro';
import Icon from '../../../components/system/Icon.astro';
import DueDiligenceView from '../../../components/pages/hub/DueDiligenceView.astro';
import LearnerVoice from '../../../components/pages/impact/LearnerVoice.astro';
import LandingLayout from '../../../layouts/LandingLayout.astro';
import HeroBand from '../../../components/sections/HeroBand.astro';
import DefinitionBand from '../../../components/sections/DefinitionBand.astro';
import ProblemBand from '../../../components/sections/ProblemBand.astro';
import MechanismBand from '../../../components/sections/MechanismBand.astro';
import CaseStudyCarousel from '../../../components/sections/CaseStudyCarousel.astro';
import IncludesBand from '../../../components/sections/IncludesBand.astro';
import FAQBand from '../../../components/sections/FAQBand.astro';
import NextStepsBand from '../../../components/sections/NextStepsBand.astro';
import CTABand from '../../../components/ui/CTABand.astro';
import SessionAgenda from '../../../components/ui/SessionAgenda.astro';
import GainsPains from '../../../components/pages/partners/GainsPains.astro';
import PartnerGuideBand from '../../../components/pages/partners/PartnerGuideBand.astro';
import SubBand from '../../../components/pages/partners/SubBand.astro';
import Callout from '../../../components/pages/partners/Callout.astro';
import OfferCards from '../../../components/pages/partners/OfferCards.astro';
import HowItWorks from '../../../components/pages/partners/HowItWorks.astro';
import Reinforcement from '../../../components/pages/partners/Reinforcement.astro';
import PageEnhancements from '../../../components/pages/partners/PageEnhancements.astro';
import DifferenceBand from '../../../components/sections/DifferenceBand.astro';
import RiskReversalBand from '../../../components/sections/RiskReversalBand.astro';
import Tone from '../../../components/pages/home/Tone.astro';
import { HOURS, KOSOVO_WOW_TILE_LABEL, LINKS, UNDP_YES_TILE_LABEL, WHERE_THEY_WORK_TEXT, funderOptionCards, meta } from '../../../components/pages/partners/partners';
import {
  C3_TRANSITION,
  DELIVERY_MODEL,
  EMPATHY,
  HEADLINE,
  METHOD,
  MICRO_COMMITMENT,
  POSITIONING,
  PROGRAM_SCOPE_STATEMENT_EXAMS,
  PROGRAM_SIZES_NOTE,
  READ_THE_EVIDENCE,
  STEPS,
  TRACK_MENU,
  TRIAL_LEAD,
  VALUE_PROPOSITION,
  WB_KOSOVO,
} from '../../../data/copy';
import { fact } from '../../../data/facts';
import { faqJsonLd } from '../../../lib/faq';
import { cta } from '../../../lib/mailto';
import { webPage } from '../../../lib/schema';
import { linkAttrs } from '../../../lib/url';

const PATH = '/partner-with-us/development-partners/';
// WP161-A (adversarial review A-02): the WebPage carries the page's own dateModified, its last visible change (it fell back to the build date). WP166-B: 2026-11-21 (the marketing review R3 changes).
const primary = cta('discovery');

const { title, description } = meta(
  'Youth Employment for Development Partners',
  // WP172 (owner decision 2026-11-27): the published study's 10 percentage points (it was the 2022 report's 10.3).
  'A randomized trial of CodersTrust’s WSDFM program: women offered a place were 10 percentage points more likely to be employed. Results you can report.',
);

// WP52 (cycle-27 review, Kimi M2): the ">4×" tile (RC-06a) showed the trial result a second time beside the "+10.3 percentage points" tile (the third reviewer in three cycles to say so), so it is gone and its comparison is the +10.3 tile's note.
// The note is read from RC-06a's glance label ("The employment effect for women offered a place was more than four times the 2.3-point average across nine vocational-training trials"), never retyped, and the build stops if the label stops ending that way.
const benchmark = /(more than four times the 2\.3-point average across nine vocational-training trials)$/.exec(fact('RC-06a').stat?.glance?.label ?? '')?.[1];
if (!benchmark) throw new Error('Development Partners: the RC-06a glance label no longer ends "... more than four times the 2.3-point average across nine vocational-training trials"; update the +10.3 tile\'s note');
const benchmarkNote = `${benchmark[0].toUpperCase()}${benchmark.slice(1)}.`;

// Q8-route: the one-line cost answer with a link to the partner hub FAQ, which keeps the full answer (WP36).
// Q6-development-partners: the women-and-marginalized-groups answer points to Proof in Practice above instead of stating its three program figures again (WP37); the bank's shared Q6 keeps the figures.
// WP152 (Opus XHigh run 6): the "How We Reach Them" card is the delivery explanation and names the facilitators trained by CodersTrust (Training of Trainers); the funding-ends answer is Q10-later ("CodersTrust-trained facilitators").
// WP166-B (marketing review R3-22): the cost answer is this page's own (Q8-development-partners: Q8-route plus the accountability commitment, ACCOUNTABILITY, which was an orphan callout).
const FAQ_IDS = ['Q1-development-partners', 'Q6-development-partners', 'Q3', 'Q10-later', 'Q8-development-partners'];

/**
 * WP175 (C8d): How It Works in Three Steps, the shared STEPS (restored from e90f9629^). The delivery model's "What We Deliver" card states the 100 hours per certification, once on the page (WP49), so step 2 leaves the
 * figure to it and opens "Training, then ..."; the build stops if STEPS[1] stops opening with the hours clause.
 */
const HOURS_CLAUSE = /^\d+ training hours per certification, including exam preparation, then /;
if (!HOURS_CLAUSE.test(STEPS[1].text)) throw new Error('Development Partners: STEPS[1] no longer opens "<hours> training hours per certification, including exam preparation, then"; update the steps');
// WP175 DRAFT: owner approval pending (step 2 without its hours clause).
const steps = [STEPS[0], { title: STEPS[1].title, text: STEPS[1].text.replace(HOURS_CLAUSE, 'Training, then ') }, STEPS[2]];
---

<LandingLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path={PATH}
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Partner with Us', href: LINKS.hub }, { label: 'Development Partners and NGOs' }]}
  jsonLd={[webPage({ path: PATH, name: title, description, dateModified: '2026-11-28' /* WP175 (owner decision Q5 of 2026-11-28): the C6 positioning statement is the crafted statement for this audience (POSITIONING in src/data/copy.ts) */ }), faqJsonLd('development-partners', PATH, FAQ_IDS)]}
  stickyCta={{ label: primary.label, href: primary.href }}
>
  <!-- C1 · Identity hook -->
  <Tone cmf="C1">
    <HeroBand
      audience="For Development Partners and NGOs"
      title={HEADLINE.developmentPartners}
      lead={VALUE_PROPOSITION.developmentPartners}
      cta={{ label: primary.label, href: primary.href }}
      artSeed="partners-development"
    />
  </Tone>
  <!-- GEO · What is CodersTrust? WP175: directly under the hero; the "How YouthWIDE Works" route link that sat between them is one of the delivery band's links now. -->
  <Tone cmf="GEO"><DefinitionBand /></Tone>
  <!-- WP175: Results at a Glance is a recorded row (doc 06 P4 v1.23), between GEO and C2. -->
  <Tone cmf="GLANCE">
    <PartnerGlance
      lead={[{ id: 'RC-02', href: '/impact/independent-evaluation/', note: benchmarkNote, also: ['RC-06a'] }, { id: 'OC-01', href: '/impact/outcomes-2026/' }, { id: 'PR-03', href: LINKS.caseUndp, label: UNDP_YES_TILE_LABEL }]}
      more={[]}
    >
      <!-- WP166-B (marketing review R3-23): the "See the Evidence Behind the Results" band was one qualifying sentence and two links the tiles above already carry; they are this note under the tiles. -->
      <p>The trial evaluated CodersTrust’s WSDFM program; the survey describes what completers who responded report about work and income.</p>
      <p class="cluster"><a class="link" href={linkAttrs(LINKS.trial).href}>Read the Trial Results<Icon name="arrow-right" size={16} /></a><a class="link" href={linkAttrs(LINKS.outcomes).href}>Impact Survey 2026<Icon name="arrow-right" size={16} /></a></p>
    </PartnerGlance>
  </Tone>

  <!-- C2–C4 · Problem, declared compressed (WP47, owner decision 2026-10-15; recast 2026-11-28): no Cost of Waiting (C3) or Imagine (C4) band; the band keeps pains P1 to P3 (data-pain, WP175) and ends with the C3
       transition line. -->
  <Tone cmf="C2-C4 (compressed)">
    <ProblemBand
      title="Show Who Reaches Paid Work"
      bluf="You can count who enrolled. Showing who is earning, and keeping hard-to-reach groups engaged, is the hard part."
      factIds={['PX-01']}
      transition={C3_TRANSITION}
    >
      <GainsPains
        slot="lead"
        pains={[
          // WP48 (cycle-23 review): "Enrollment targets are often met, while employment results lag behind." was a claim about the whole sector; the card says only what the page can stand behind.
          // WP49 (cycle-24 review): the two card titles were broad sector conclusions too ("Skills Programs Miss Their Employment Targets", "The Hardest-to-Reach Groups Drop Out"), as was the band heading; they say what the partner needs, and the bodies stay.
          // WP60 (cycle-32 review, Gemini GM6): the card's body is the need, "Connect training to a first job, freelance client or business." (it said "Enrollment is easy to count. Paid work is not.", which repeated the band's lead above it).
          // WP114 (Opus Low run 6): the body states the reader's obstacle, not an instruction (the card band describes obstacles).
          { id: 'P1', q: 'Training must connect to paid work', text: 'Training often ends before a first job, freelance client or business.' },
          { id: 'P2', q: 'Barriers can interrupt training', text: 'Computer skills, devices, connectivity and social barriers can keep learners from joining or staying.' },
          { id: 'P3', q: 'Outcomes are hard to audit', text: 'Without a baseline and documentary evidence, evaluators have little to check.' },
        ]}
      />
    </ProblemBand>
  </Tone>

  <!-- C5 · Guide. WP175 (Q2 (a)): empathy (EMPATHY.developmentPartners, a fresh line: WP44 had removed the doc 04 line) → the one-line origin (the SC-03 tile carries the count, so the line names the regions, WP150) →
       authority (the SC-01 and SC-03 tiles, the signed agreements, the world strip, the logo strip) → the method line (METHOD.developmentPartners, which WP166-B R3-29 had taken out) as the band's last line. -->
  <Tone cmf="C5">
    <PartnerGuideBand
      empathy={EMPATHY.developmentPartners}
      stats={['SC-01', 'SC-03']}
      signed="SC-11c"
      partnerGroup="development"
      strip
      method={METHOD.developmentPartners}
      methodLast
    />
  </Tone>

  <!-- C6 · Mechanism (YouthWIDE), the positioning statement first. WP166-B (marketing review R3-24): the approach lead closes with the page's one delivery-model sentence (DELIVERY_MODEL; it led the first "What Your Portfolio Gets" card).
       WP175: "How YouthWIDE Works" (the route link that sat under the hero) is a second link beside "How Our Model Works". -->
  <Tone cmf="C6">
    <MechanismBand
      title="An Integrated Path from Training to Earnings"
      positioning={POSITIONING.developmentPartners}
      lead={`YouthWIDE is the program through which funders deploy the CodersTrust model in a country; it builds on results from earlier programs in Bangladesh and Kosovo. ${DELIVERY_MODEL.text}`}
      blocks={[
        { label: 'Who We Reach', title: 'Priority Groups', text: 'Priority groups are agreed with you in co-design; women are a priority in every program.', icon: 'users' },
        { label: 'How We Reach Them', title: 'Existing Labs and Colleges', text: 'Outreach and selection with local partners, then blended delivery by facilitators trained by CodersTrust (Training of Trainers).', icon: 'graduation-cap' },
        { label: 'What We Deliver', title: 'Certification Preparation and Mentoring', text: `${HOURS} training hours per certification, including exam preparation, then three months of milestone-based mentoring and placement support.`, icon: 'award' },
        { label: 'Where They Work', title: 'Local, Remote and Global Work', text: WHERE_THEY_WORK_TEXT, icon: 'globe' },
      ]}
      link={{ label: 'How Our Model Works', href: LINKS.model }}
      moreLinks={[{ label: 'How YouthWIDE Works', href: LINKS.youthwide }]}
    />
  </Tone>

  <!-- C7 · Proof. WP175 (Q3 (a)): the case-study carousel leads with the one-line trial lead (TRIAL_LEAD); the trial and survey tiles stay in the glance above (WP166-B, R3-23). Adrian Afraham's story follows the carousel,
       inside C7 (it sat between the glance and the problem band). -->
  <Tone cmf="C7">
    <CaseStudyCarousel
      title="Proof in Practice"
      lead={TRIAL_LEAD.text}
      items={[
        { title: 'Women’s Skill Development for Freelancing Marketplace (WSDFM)', program: 'Commissioned by Porticus', factId: 'PR-01', href: LINKS.caseWsdfm },
        { title: 'Women in Online Work, Kosovo', program: 'World Bank Group', factId: 'PR-02', statLabel: KOSOVO_WOW_TILE_LABEL, note: WB_KOSOVO, href: LINKS.caseWow },
        { title: 'Her Power', program: 'ICT Division / Department of ICT (DoICT)', factId: 'PR-05', href: LINKS.caseHerPower },
      ]}
    />
    <LearnerVoice story="adrian-afraham" excerpt="Following my completion of the course, I successfully secured my very first freelance job, which was followed by an enticing job offer." program="Graphic Designing course, CodersTrust Bangladesh" tone="white" summary={false} />
  </Tone>

  <!-- C8 · Features to benefits. WP39 (cycle-16 review): three short cards in place of the concern / inclusion / benefit matrix, in the order of the three pains in C2 (training must connect to paid work; barriers can interrupt training;
       outcomes are hard to audit). Each sentence rests on the register: PD-01 (mentoring and placement support alongside the training; WP49, cycle-24 review: the first card says only that CodersTrust connects them in one program (WP65 wording), because the 100 hours
       per certification are in the delivery model above), PD-02 and PD-10 (outreach and selection, tracks from employer demand), PD-12 (safeguarding specified per program), PD-08 and PD-09 (monthly reports, baseline, follow-up surveys,
       documentary evidence where available). WP175: each card carries the pain it relieves (data-relieves); the gain creator is doc 04 4.1's first (certification exam preparation → a supported route to a recognized credential);
       How It Works in Three Steps follows the cards (restored from e90f9629^). -->
  <Tone cmf="C8">
    <IncludesBand
      title="What Your Portfolio Gets"
      cards={[
        // WP163 (owner answers of 3 November 2026): one exam fee per trainee is included, any further attempts set per program, and the card states the delivery model once on this page (DELIVERY_MODEL).
        // WP166-B (marketing review R3-24, R3-05): the delivery-model sentence is in How It Works; the delivery card names the track menu once (TRACK_MENU, PD-10).
        { icon: 'route', title: 'Training, Mentoring and Placement Support', text: 'Certification exam preparation and one exam fee per trainee (any further attempts set per program), three months of mentoring and placement support in one program.', painId: 'P1', facts: ['PD-01', 'PD-02a'] },
        { icon: 'map-pin', title: 'Accessible Local Delivery', text: `Outreach, accessible selection and tracks chosen from employer demand, with safeguarding agreed for each program. ${TRACK_MENU.text}`, painId: 'P2', facts: ['PD-02', 'PD-10', 'PD-12'] },
        { icon: 'clipboard-check', title: 'Progress Your Evaluators Can Check', text: 'Baseline data, follow-up surveys and monthly reports, with documentary evidence where available.', painId: 'P3', facts: ['PD-08', 'PD-09'] },
        // doc 04 4.1 gain creator, in doc 06's words for it ("each track prepares learners for an industry certification → A supported route to a recognized credential"); it names no vendor, so no CertNote
        { icon: 'badge-check', title: 'A Supported Route to a Recognized Credential', text: 'Each track prepares learners for an industry certification exam.', facts: ['PD-02', 'PD-03'] },
      ]}
      scope={PROGRAM_SCOPE_STATEMENT_EXAMS}
    />
    <HowItWorks steps={steps} />
  </Tone>

  <!-- C9 · Objections, with the due-diligence view (governance; WP34, cycle-11 review: one item and a link stand in for the four diligence cards). WP175: it sat between the offer and the call to action. -->
  <Tone cmf="C9">
    <FAQBand
      audience="development-partners"
      ids={FAQ_IDS}
      title="Questions Partners Ask"
    />
    <DueDiligenceView brief id="due-diligence" />
  </Tone>

  <!-- C10 · Differentiation (WP175, owner decision of 2026-11-28): the short band, doc 04 4.1's USPs 1, 2 and 4 (USP 3, the credential, is the C8 gain creator above); no comparison table (WP47). -->
  <Tone cmf="C10"><DifferenceBand usps={['evidence', 'earners', 'infrastructure']} /></Tone>

  <!-- C11 · Offer. WP166-B (marketing review R3-22): the "Accountability" callout is the cost answer's second sentence in the FAQ above (Q8-development-partners). -->
  <Tone cmf="C11">
    <OfferCards eyebrow="Options" title="Choose Where to Start" lead={PROGRAM_SIZES_NOTE} cards={funderOptionCards()} recommended={0} />
  </Tone>

  <!-- C12 · Risk reversal, immediately before C13 (WP175: restored; RiskReversalBand had been removed in WP17, 948453bd). Doc 04 4.1 C12 "Start with a pilot. Decide on evidence.", in the Title Case form Universities and YouthWIDE
       print, with the band's default points (pilot review, scorecard thresholds, the funder's decision, independent evaluation). The "Reporting and Review" callout (WP45, WP40: the one place the page names the quarterly
       reviews, tied to PD-08 and PD-11) that stood in for the band stays, tucked under it. -->
  <Tone cmf="C12">
    <RiskReversalBand title="Start with a Pilot, Then Decide on Evidence" />
    <SubBand pull>
      <Callout eyebrow="Reporting and Review">
        <p data-fact="PD-08 PD-11">For supported cohorts, monthly reports track progress; quarterly reviews guide corrective action and any expansion.</p>
      </Callout>
    </SubBand>
  </Tone>

  <!-- C13 · Call to action -->
  <Tone cmf="C13">
    <CTABand
      id="cta"
      heading="Let’s Design Your First Cohort"
      cta={{ label: primary.label, href: primary.href }}
      secondary={{ label: READ_THE_EVIDENCE, href: LINKS.evidence }}
      micro={MICRO_COMMITMENT}
    >
      <SessionAgenda />
    </CTABand>
  </Tone>

  <!-- C14 · Next steps -->
  <!-- WP46 (cycle-22 review): the grant-funded timing (training starts 6–8 weeks after signing, procurement on the agency's own timeline; WP100: nothing is said about the time before signing) is stated once on this page, in the steps and lead below; the C13 urgency line that repeated it is gone (as on Foundations, whose steps band is the same). -->
  <Tone cmf="C14">
    <NextStepsBand timing={false} />
    <Reinforcement logos={false} reminder={false} />
  </Tone>

  <PageEnhancements />
</LandingLayout>
